WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.220
Git events often trigger automation: pushes, pull requests, tags, releases, schedules, webhooks, manual dispatches, and dependency updates.

2
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.840
These triggers do not all have the same trust level.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:24.140
A push to a protected branch, a pull request from a fork, a dependency update, and a release tag should not automatically receive the same credentials or authority.

4
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.340
CI/CD design should separate validation, build, publish, and deployment jobs.

5
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.800
Validation can often run with little or no secret access.

6
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.420
Build jobs may need read access to dependencies.

7
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.080
Publish jobs may need package credentials.

8
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.340
Deployment jobs may need environment-specific authority and approvals.

9
00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:50.760
Splitting these roles reduces blast radius and makes it easier to reason about which event can trigger which action.

10
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.920
Low-trust triggers should not receive production secrets.

11
00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:02.260
A pull request from a less trusted source should not be able to reach privileged deployment paths merely because it changes a workflow file or runs a job.

12
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:13.000
Branch filters, protected tags, protected environments, approval gates, and scoped secrets help keep high-impact actions behind reviewed workflows.

13
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.160
The goal is to run helpful automation without letting every trigger become a privileged pathway.

14
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:23.220
Automation should provide fast feedback while protecting release and deployment authority.

15
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:30.360
If a trigger cannot be trusted with a secret or credential, design the workflow so it can still validate safely without receiving that authority.

16
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.340
Privilege should be tied to trust, review, and environment scope.
